# Patch files are applied with `git apply` inside the Linux container, against
# sources npm just wrote with LF endings. A patch checked out with CRLF has a
# trailing \r on every context line, matches nothing, and fails as "patch does
# not apply" — which reads like a stale patch set rather than a checkout
# artefact. On Windows core.autocrlf=true is the default that git for Windows
# installs, so without this line a fresh clone there cannot build at all.
# -text: store and check these out byte for byte, whatever the platform.
*.patch -text

# Everything the container executes as a script has the same requirement: a
# shebang line ending in \r makes the kernel look for an interpreter that does
# not exist ("bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M").
*.sh text eol=lf
